---
<DocPage eyebrow="TOOLS / VALUE" title="Value tools" lead="gua-value-tools is a TypeScript library that validates values from a game and compares them. Use it in inspection UIs or tests that read additional game observations." description="Practical gua-value-tools use cases, inputs, outputs, installation, and comparison rules." section="docs">
  <h2>Example: check the inventory contains the expected items</h2><p>Compare a game’s JSON saying “key and map are held” with an expected set. parseValue takes the original JSON string and returns a validated value; valuesEqual returns whether the contents match. Invalid input raises ValueError.</p>
  <CodeBlock title="Compare sets with a different item order" lang="ts" code={"import { parseValue, valuesEqual } from \"gua-value-tools\";\n\nconst actual = parseValue('{\"type\":\"set\",\"elementType\":\"string\",\"value\":[\"key\",\"map\"]}');\nconst expected = parseValue('{\"type\":\"set\",\"elementType\":\"string\",\"value\":[\"map\",\"key\"]}');\nconsole.log(valuesEqual(actual, expected)); // true"} />
  <p>The game registers values outside the standard UI as Observe values: additional published observations. gua-value-tools handles received values; it does not register them or connect to the game. <a href="/docs/gua-runtime/#observe">Register observations</a> · <a href="/docs/npm-packages/">Combine npm packages</a></p>
  <h2>Install the npm package</h2>
  <CodeBlock title="Pin the 1.1.0 package" lang="powershell" code={`npm install gua-value-tools@1.1.0`} />
  <p><a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/gua-value-tools">gua-value-tools</a> provides TypeScript types and validation, JSON parsing/serialization, enum catalogs, and semantic equality.</p>
  <p>An enum is a value chosen from named alternatives. If a boss phase is First or Second, register those names in an EnumCatalog. Validation rejects unknown members or the wrong type.</p>
  <CodeBlock title="Parse, validate, and compare an enum" lang="ts" code={`import { EnumCatalog, parseValue, serializeValue, valuesEqual } from "gua-value-tools";

const catalog = new EnumCatalog();
catalog.register("game.BossPhase", ["First", "Second"]);
const value = parseValue(
  '{"type":"enum","enumType":"game.BossPhase","value":"Second"}', catalog);
console.log(serializeValue(value, catalog));
console.log(valuesEqual(value, value, catalog));`} />
  <h2>Types and equality</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Contract</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td><code>bool</code>, <code>string</code></td><td>Booleans and valid Unicode scalar strings; no case folding or Unicode normalization.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>integer</code></td><td>Mathematical integers from −9007199254740991 to 9007199254740991, checked before binary64 rounding.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>number</code></td><td>Finite binary64; distinct from integer even for the same numeric value.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>enum</code></td><td>Explicit registered enum type and member name. Aliases with different names are different values.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>list</code></td><td>Ordered homogeneous scalar elements; duplicates allowed.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>set</code></td><td>Homogeneous scalar elements; order ignored for equality and duplicates rejected. Serialization preserves input order.</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>
  <p>Empty collections retain <code>elementType</code> and any required <code>enumType</code>. Null, arbitrary objects, object references, nested collections, BigInt, NaN, and Infinity are forbidden. Different types are unequal; invalid values raise a validation error. Use <code>valuesEqual</code> rather than comparing serialized JSON bytes.</p>
  <Callout kind="warn" title="Parse the original JSON string"><p>Pass external JSON directly to <code>parseValue</code>. Calling <code>JSON.parse</code> first can lose integer precision before validation. <code>createValue</code> validates JavaScript values and returns copied, frozen data, but cannot recover precision already lost. <code>ValueError</code> exposes a code and path.</p></Callout>
  <h2>Enum catalog lifetime</h2>
  <p>Register a dotted explicit ID such as <code>game.BossPhase</code> and a nonempty set of unique member names. Re-registering the same ID with the same members succeeds regardless of member order; changing the member set is rejected. Use a new ID for a changed definition. Pass the corresponding catalog for enum validation, output, and equality, including empty enum collections. Catalogs are not global and do not infer IDs from language type names.</p>
  <h2>Native and .NET consumers</h2>
  <p>The same contract is available through <code>gua/value.h</code>, C++ <code>gua::Value</code>/<code>gua::EnumCatalog</code>, and <code>Gua.Core.GuaValue</code>/<code>GuaEnumCatalog</code>. C++ and .NET delegate to the native C ABI. The TypeScript package provides the same typed-value semantics.</p>
  <Callout title="How this relates to UI and World values"><p>Value v1 does not change legacy UI <code>value</code>, World <code>state</code>, or Input vector2 wire formats. Additional Observe values use their own contract and negotiated <code>observe_v1</code> capability. See <a href="/docs/gua-runtime/#observe">Observe registration and subscriptions</a>.</p></Callout>
  <p>Sources pinned to 1.1.0: <a href="https://github.com/gua-project/gua/blob/gua-v1.1.0/packages/value/README.md">package API</a>, <a href="https://github.com/gua-project/gua/blob/gua-v1.1.0/protocol/specs/value-v1.md">Value semantics and ABI</a>, and <a href="https://github.com/gua-project/gua/blob/gua-v1.1.0/protocol/schema/value-v1.schema.json">schema</a>.</p>
</DocPage>
